    "question": {
      ":question/id": "8d433279-8b94-5f88-9855-5c59098cb67f",
      ":question/problem": "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=6(t-4),\\,y=2{t}^{3}-18t,\\,-\\infty <t<\\infty .\n$$\n\nDetermine the values of $t$ at which the normal to the curve is vertical.",
      ":question/worked-solution": "The normal is vertical if the tangent is horizontal. Therefore, we require ${y}^{′}(t)=0$ while ${x}^{′}(t)\\ne 0.$\n\nSo, we first find the derivatives of $x$ and $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=6,\\,{y}^{′}(t)=6{t}^{2}-18.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving ${y}^{′}(t)=0$ gives\n\n$$\n6({t}^{2}-3)=0\\,⟹\\,t=\\pm \\sqrt{3}.\n$$\n\nNow, we note that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(-\\sqrt{3}) & =6\\ne 0, \\\\ {x}^{′}(\\sqrt{3}) & =6\\ne 0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich confirms that the normal to the curve is indeed vertical when $t=\\pm \\sqrt{3}.$",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/moderate",
