  {
    "knowledge_point": {
      "db_id": 17592186083280,
      "id": "12a5ad95-0cc9-53c5-8e1c-dae35d1c4447",
      "title": "Determining the Points Where the Normal is Horizontal or Vertical"
    },
    "question": {
      ":question/id": "4d7bfcb6-723b-5bc8-a16f-500e0073bdff",
      ":question/problem": "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t-\\frac{\\pi }{4})+0,\\,y=6\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t-\\frac{\\pi }{4})+7,\\,0\\le t<2\\pi .\n$$\n\nDetermine the values of $t$ at which the normal to the curve is vertical. Give exact answers.\n\nIn **ascending order**, the normal to the curve is vertical at $t=$\n{{field-1}}\nand $t=$\n{{field-2}}.",
      ":question/worked-solution": "The normal is vertical if the tangent is horizontal. Therefore, we require ${y}^{′}(t)=0$ while ${x}^{′}(t)\\ne 0.$\n\nSo, we first find the derivatives of $x$ and $y:$\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t-\\frac{\\pi }{4}),\\,{y}^{′}(t)=6\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t-\\frac{\\pi }{4})\n$$\n\nSolving ${y}^{′}(t)=0$ gives\n\n$$\n6\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t-\\frac{\\pi }{4})=0\\,⟹\\,t=\\frac{3\\pi }{4},\\frac{7\\pi }{4}.\n$$\n\nNow, we note that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(\\frac{3\\pi }{4}) & =-2\\ne 0, \\\\ {x}^{′}(\\frac{7\\pi }{4}) & =2\\ne 0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich confirms that the normal to the curve is indeed vertical when $t=\\frac{3\\pi }{4}$ and $t=\\frac{7\\pi }{4}.$",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/moderate",
      ":question/answer-fields": [
        {
          ":answer-field/id": "c523d5e8-15bb-583a-a48d-71050082008f",
          ":answer-field/key": "field-2",
          ":answer-field/type": ":answer-field.type/blank",
          ":answer-field/choices": [
            {
              ":answer/id": "0c78372c-899b-5c21-a56e-71b629f84a8b",
              ":answer/type": ":answer.type/math",
              ":answer/value": "\\frac{7\\pi }{4}"
            }
          ],
          ":answer-field/correct": [
