    "question": {
      ":question/id": "ab08d4bd-e66f-5a03-9399-676156155de6",
      ":question/problem": "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=3(t-2),\\,y=2{t}^{3}-60t,\\,-\\infty <t<\\infty .\n$$\n\nDetermine the values of $t$ at which the normal to the curve is vertical.",
      ":question/worked-solution": "The normal is vertical if the tangent is horizontal. Therefore, we require ${y}^{′}(t)=0$ while ${x}^{′}(t)\\ne 0.$\n\nSo, we first find the derivatives of $x$ and $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=3,\\,{y}^{′}(t)=6{t}^{2}-60.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving ${y}^{′}(t)=0$ gives\n\n$$\n6({t}^{2}-10)=0\\,⟹\\,t=\\pm \\sqrt{10}.\n$$\n\nNow, we note that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(-\\sqrt{10}) & =3\\ne 0, \\\\ {x}^{′}(\\sqrt{10}) & =3\\ne 0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich confirms that the normal to the curve is indeed vertical when $t=\\pm \\sqrt{10}.$",
