      ":question/problem": "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=9(t-1),\\,y=3{t}^{3}-63t,\\,-\\infty <t<\\infty .\n$$\n\nDetermine the values of $t$ at which the normal to the curve is vertical.",
      ":question/worked-solution": "The normal is vertical if the tangent is horizontal. Therefore, we require ${y}^{′}(t)=0$ while ${x}^{′}(t)\\ne 0.$\n\nSo, we first find the derivatives of $x$ and $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=9,\\,{y}^{′}(t)=9{t}^{2}-63.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving ${y}^{′}(t)=0$ gives\n\n$$\n9({t}^{2}-7)=0\\,⟹\\,t=\\pm \\sqrt{7}.\n$$\n\nNow, we note that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(-\\sqrt{7}) & =9\\ne 0, \\\\ {x}^{′}(\\sqrt{7}) & =9\\ne 0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich confirms that the normal to the curve is indeed vertical when $t=\\pm \\sqrt{7}.$",
