    },
    "question": {
      ":question/id": "917f4834-108a-55b8-bdf4-2b432ca752b7",
      ":question/problem": "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=8(t-3),\\,y=1{t}^{3}-33t,\\,-\\infty <t<\\infty .\n$$\n\nDetermine the values of $t$ at which the normal to the curve is vertical.",
      ":question/worked-solution": "The normal is vertical if the tangent is horizontal. Therefore, we require ${y}^{′}(t)=0$ while ${x}^{′}(t)\\ne 0.$\n\nSo, we first find the derivatives of $x$ and $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=8,\\,{y}^{′}(t)=3{t}^{2}-33.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving ${y}^{′}(t)=0$ gives\n\n$$\n3({t}^{2}-11)=0\\,⟹\\,t=\\pm \\sqrt{11}.\n$$\n\nNow, we note that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(-\\sqrt{11}) & =8\\ne 0, \\\\ {x}^{′}(\\sqrt{11}) & =8\\ne 0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich confirms that the normal to the curve is indeed vertical when $t=\\pm \\sqrt{11}.$",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/moderate",
      ":question/answer-fields": [
        {
          ":answer-field/id": "43bdab77-86f6-50c5-ad05-854430b4bc99",
